{"success": true, "message": "Test details sent successfully", "data": {"test": {"_id": "69de21eb7e39d99b57bc5ab0", "analysisVideoCdn": "yt", "exam": ["605dfcb2f226ac25a8bd5fef"], "ctype": null, "position": 0, "order": 12, "enableCalculator": false, "isContinuousQuestion": false, "reattemptAvailable": false, "singleSectionMultipleSubjects": false, "timeRestricted": false, "title": "Gravitation - Test 4", "description": "Previous Year Questions as Chapter Test", "syllabus": "This test contains questions from all topics of this chapter included in the JEE Main syllabus. It includes a mix of questions from PYQs of JEE Main 2019 - 2025.", "totalTime": 50, "totalQuestions": 25, "totalMarks": 100, "bonusQuestions": null, "bonusMarks": null, "startDate": "2026-04-15T03:30:00.000Z", "endDate": "2027-04-30T00:00:00.000Z", "type": "free", "class": "605df635a37b7025c438d685", "instruction": "608289ffb160b022e9442c83", "subjects": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "totalMarks": 100, "bonusMarks": 0, "totalQuestions": 25, "bonusQuestions": 0, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d92901b9"}], "sections": [{"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d92901b5", "title": "Physics Single Correct", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290133", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["6117b60a478ae845c43c63f1"], "concepts": ["6117b607478ae845c43c62fb"], "question": {"text": "<p><strong>Statement I :</strong> The law of gravitation holds good for any pair of bodies in the universe.</p><p><strong>Statement II :</strong> The weight of any person becomes zero when the person is at the centre of the earth.</p><p>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "0ec21f", "text": "Statement&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;and statement&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;both are true.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290134"}, {"id": "fa5e4f", "text": "Statement&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;is true and statement&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;is false.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290135"}, {"id": "9ed842", "text": "Statement&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;and statement&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;both are false.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290136"}, {"id": "086ff9", "text": "Statement&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;is false and statement&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;is true.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290137"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290138", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927a"], "concepts": ["60e567fa499d1e49588fa01b"], "question": {"text": "The value of acceleration due to gravity at Earth&#39;s surface is <math><mi>&#160;</mi><mn>9.8</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math> . The altitude above its surface at which the acceleration due to gravity decreases to <math><mi>&#160;</mi><mn>4.9</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>,&nbsp;is close to: (Radius of earth <math><mi>&#160;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6.4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> )"}, "options": [{"id": "c9cd4a", "text": "<math><mn>1.6</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290139"}, {"id": "5a90e7", "text": "<math><mn>2.6</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929013a"}, {"id": "f734e5", "text": "<math><mn>6.4</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929013b"}, {"id": "d22675", "text": "<math><mn>9.0</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929013c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929013d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b6b"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b2f"], "question": {"text": "To project a body of mass $m$ from earth's surface to infinity, the required kinetic energy is (assume, the radius of earth is $R_E, g=$ acceleration due to gravity on the surface of earth):"}, "options": [{"id": "391a98", "text": "$2 m g R_E$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929013e"}, {"id": "6dbc26", "text": "$4 m g R_E$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929013f"}, {"id": "3bf850", "text": "$m g R_E$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290140"}, {"id": "9744ee", "text": "$1 / 2 m g R_E$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290141"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290142", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f87fd"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87c5"], "question": {"text": "Two satellites of masses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>&nbsp;revolve around the earth in circular orbits of radii <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></math> respectively. The ratio of orbital speeds of the satellites respectively is"}, "options": [{"id": "12ac48", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290143"}, {"id": "34e7de", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290144"}, {"id": "c12d1a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290145"}, {"id": "aa9d34", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290146"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290147", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4763"], "concepts": ["60e40fd4f1ef260f84ed1e41"], "question": {"text": "The time period of a satellite in a circular orbit of the radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi><mo>.</mo></math> The period of another satellite in a circular orbit of the radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn><mi>R</mi></math> is:"}, "options": [{"id": "699fc0", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn><mi>T</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290148"}, {"id": "f93e18", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>27</mn><mi>T</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290149"}, {"id": "e2ad57", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mi>T</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929014a"}, {"id": "ab8bbf", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi>T</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929014b"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929014c", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b6b"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52bb"], "question": {"text": "On the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math>-axis and at a distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> from the origin, the gravitational field due to a mass distribution is given by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><msup><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></math>&nbsp;in the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math>-direction. The magnitude of the gravitational potential on the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math>-axis at a distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math>, taking its value to be zero at infinity is:"}, "options": [{"id": "3e6ede", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>A</mi><msup><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929014d"}, {"id": "9ccb5a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>A</mi><msup><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929014e"}, {"id": "13b6c9", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><msup><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929014f"}, {"id": "3299d1", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><msup><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290150"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290151", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f87fd"], "concepts": ["60e56812499d1e49588fa38a"], "question": {"text": "Consider two satellites <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> with periods of revolution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mi>hr</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mi>hr</mi></math> respectively revolving around a planet in circular orbits. The ratio of angular velocity of satellite <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> to the angular velocity of satellite <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is:"}, "options": [{"id": "53e3bf", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290152"}, {"id": "0cd97f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290153"}, {"id": "df6d34", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>4</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290154"}, {"id": "749afc", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>8</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290155"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290156", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["6117b60a478ae845c43c63f1"], "concepts": ["6117b8bd67dbe2603c3e85a2"], "question": {"text": "<p>A planet has double the mass of the earth. Its average density is equal to that of the earth. An object weighing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>W</mi></math> on earth will weigh on that planet:</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "c0f196", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290157"}, {"id": "52f473", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290158"}, {"id": "e48317", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290159"}, {"id": "c0fafd", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929015a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929015b", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927a"], "concepts": ["6162faf16ed13040ec849361"], "question": {"text": "<p>At what distance above and below the surface of the earth a body will have same weight? (Take radius of earth as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math>)</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "29e33e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929015c"}, {"id": "b687aa", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi>R</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929015d"}, {"id": "491a3f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929015e"}, {"id": "57dc25", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mi>R</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929015f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290160", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4763"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87cb"], "question": {"text": "The angular momentum of a planet of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi></math> moving around the sun in an elliptical orbit is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>L</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover></math>. The magnitude of the areal velocity of the planet is :"}, "options": [{"id": "d30ce1", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>L</mi></mrow><mi>M</mi></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290161"}, {"id": "83fba6", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>L</mi><mi>M</mi></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290162"}, {"id": "9b2dcc", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></mrow><mi>M</mi></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290163"}, {"id": "c0cc6f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>L</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>M</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290164"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290165", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927a"], "concepts": ["60e5678c499d1e49588f9251"], "question": {"text": "<p>Given below are two statements:</p><p><strong>Statement I</strong> : Rotation of the earth shows effect on the value of acceleration due to gravity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>)</mo></math>.</p><p><strong>Statement II</strong> : The effect of rotation of the earth on the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi></math>&nbsp;at the equator is minimum and that at the pole is maximum.</p><p>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "d3a6dd", "text": "Statement I is false but statement II is true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290166"}, {"id": "d0b9bb", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290167"}, {"id": "ab0a17", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290168"}, {"id": "5d41d9", "text": "Statement I is true but statement II is false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290169"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929016a", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f87fd"], "concepts": ["60f34752eff4df09e43c25d2"], "question": {"text": "A satellite of $10^3 \\mathrm{~kg}$ mass is revolving in circular orbit of radius $2 R$. If $\\frac{10^4 R}{6} J$ energy is supplied to the satellite, it would revolve in a new circular orbit of radius\n(use $g=10 \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s}^2, R=$ radius of earth)"}, "options": [{"id": "f4e899", "text": "$2.5 R$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929016b"}, {"id": "a58d29", "text": "$3 R$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929016c"}, {"id": "b3aa12", "text": "$4 R$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929016d"}, {"id": "7a9502", "text": "$6 R$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929016e"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929016f", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b6b"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8ed7"], "question": {"text": "<p>A body is projected vertically upwards from the surface of earth with a velocity sufficient enough to carry it to infinity. The time taken by it to reach height <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo></menclose></math>&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "1ca76d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mfenced><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290170"}, {"id": "c57caf", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mi>g</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mfenced><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290171"}, {"id": "aa4451", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><msqrt><mfrac><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mfenced><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290172"}, {"id": "e99382", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mi>g</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mfenced><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290173"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290174", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["6117b60a478ae845c43c63f1"], "concepts": ["6117b607478ae845c43c62fb"], "question": {"text": "A straight ro