ac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290178"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290179", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4763"], "concepts": ["60e40fd4f1ef260f84ed1e41"], "question": {"text": "The distance between Sun and Earth is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>. The duration of year if the distance between Sun and Earth becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi>R</mi></math> will be :"}, "options": [{"id": "feafa1", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>yr</mi></math>&nbsp;", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929017a"}, {"id": "8dab58", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>yr</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929017b"}, {"id": "ecb2e1", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>yr</mi></math>&nbsp;", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929017c"}, {"id": "2fa19f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>yr</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929017d"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929017e", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927a"], "concepts": ["60e567fa499d1e49588fa01b"], "question": {"text": "The acceleration due to gravity on the earth&#39;s surface at the poles is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi></math> and angular velocity of the earth about the axis passing through the pole is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#969;</mi></math>. An object is weighed at the equator and at a height <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi></math> above the poles by using a spring balance. If the weights are found to be same, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi></math> is: (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi><mo>&#8810;</mo><mi>R</mi></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> is the radius of the earth)"}, "options": [{"id": "9c5409", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>&#969;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d929017f"}, {"id": "7adb89", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>&#969;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>g</mi></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290180"}, {"id": "4d2000", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>&#969;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290181"}, {"id": "3aa640", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>&#969;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290182"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f7f2ee0e063d9290183", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b6b"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b2f"], "question": {"text": "A body is projected vertically upwards from the surface of earth with a velocity equal to one third of escape velocity. The maximum height attained by the body will be<br />(Take radius of earth 